honey danber uucp is NOT public domain, nor has it been posted to the net.
It is a rewrite of uucp by Peter Honeyman, Dave Nowitz, and Brian Redman,
and is available from AT&T.   My lab has about 5 computers; switching
from old-uucp to HDB has saved us about 1 full-time administrator
because uucp is now reliable.  It also knows how to use a variety of
transmission media, including Datakit and Sytek, and supports most
popular autodialing modems (i.e. there's a Dialers file, and you can
add anything you don't have.)

There are some minor problems - it can
do multihop uucp, and so can System V, but the two aren't compatible.
However, multihop never worked in the past, and most people around here
use HDB instead of old-uucp, so nothing broke.
